WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.610
I typed “See you” and my phone

2
00:00:03.610 --> 00:00:05.490
suggested “tomorrow”.

3
00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:08.730
Then a chatbot wrote me a

4
00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:10.770
whole poem about fractions.

5
00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:13.800
Not a bad one, either.

6
00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:16.800
Same trick, just bigger?

7
00:00:18.130 --> 00:00:21.210
Today: how a chatbot writes, and

8
00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:22.650
why that matters for you.

9
00:00:23.830 --> 00:00:26.790
Inside every chatbot is a language model.

10
00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:29.430
It was trained on a huge amount

11
00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:31.590
of text: books, websites,

12
00:00:32.170 --> 00:00:33.590
articles and conversations.

13
00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:35.980
That’s more than any person

14
00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:37.760
could read in many lifetimes.

15
00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:40.310
From all of it, it learned which

16
00:00:40.310 --> 00:00:41.890
words tend to follow which.

17
00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:45.060
Then it writes like your phone’s

18
00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:46.880
keyboard, only far better.

19
00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:50.420
It looks at everything written so far, and

20
00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:52.620
scores every possible next piece.

21
00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:55.640
It picks a likely one, adds it,

22
00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:56.900
and does it all again.

23
00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:00.280
Piece by piece, a whole answer appears.

24
00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:03.880
So, a language model is a

25
00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:05.900
program that predicts the next

26
00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:08.140
piece of text, over and over.

27
00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:11.540
These pieces are called tokens: a short

28
00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:13.540
word, or part of a longer one.

29
00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:16.360
On its own, it doesn’t look facts up.

30
00:01:16.970 --> 00:01:19.030
It writes what usually comes next.

31
00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:21.760
It’s like a friend who has

32
00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:23.560
heard every story in town.

33
00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:26.430
Start with “Once upon a” and they

34
00:01:26.430 --> 00:01:28.450
say “time” before you finish.

35
00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:30.930
They’re usually right, because

36
00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:32.290
they’ve heard it so often.

37
00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:34.460
But ask about something they’ve

38
00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:36.500
never heard, and they may still

39
00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:38.660
answer, smoothly, with a guess.

40
00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:40.570
Quick check!

41
00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:43.980
A chatbot answers in perfect,

42
00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:45.880
confident sentences.

43
00:01:46.690 --> 00:01:49.270
Does that mean its facts are right?

44
00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:52.760
Not necessarily: fluent

45
00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:54.400
isn’t the same as true.

46
00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:56.720
It’s built to write what sounds

47
00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:58.980
likely, not to check what’s true.

48
00:01:59.510 --> 00:02:01.830
So a made-up date comes out just

49
00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:03.210
as smoothly as a real one.

50
00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:06.280
And what it learned is uneven.

51
00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:09.740
There’s far more text online in English

52
00:02:09.740 --> 00:02:14.300
than in Arabic, Hausa or Swahili, and more

53
00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:16.780
about big cities than small towns.

54
00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:20.120
So it may know less about your country and

55
00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:22.360
your language, and guess more there.

56
00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:25.620
Most models also stopped learning on a

57
00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:28.660
fixed date, so recent news can be missing.

58
00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:32.270
In the app, you’ll train a tiny language

59
00:02:32.270 --> 00:02:35.050
model on sixteen short sentences about

60
00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:37.790
school, like “The pupils open their

61
00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:38.270
books”.

62
00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:41.840
Then let it write, one word at a time.

63
00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:44.300
You’ll see its choices for each

64
00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:46.320
next word, and whether its

65
00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:48.420
sentence is new or just copied.

66
00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:50.450
Your turn.

67
00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:53.790
My tiny model learned sixteen

68
00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:55.350
sentences about school.

69
00:02:56.030 --> 00:02:58.810
Can it write a story about the market?

70
00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:02.040
No: it can only write from what it

71
00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:04.520
learned, and a big chatbot is the

72
00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:06.540
same, with far more text.

73
00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:09.360
Remember: it predicts the next

74
00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:11.400
piece, again and again.

75
00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:13.930
Fluent isn’t the same as true.

76
00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:16.420
And it may know less about your

77
00:03:16.420 --> 00:03:17.840
country and your language.

78
00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:21.150
Try it yourself: play “One

79
00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:22.210
word each” with a friend.

80
00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:25.500
Take turns adding the most likely next

81
00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:27.940
word to a story, without planning ahead.

82
00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:31.440
Stop at twenty words and read it aloud.

83
00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:34.620
Where did it start to go wrong, and why?
